<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sertorian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">80–<a href="/years/72-bce">72 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sertorian War was a Roman civil war fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> from 80 to 72 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a>, a supporter of the Marian party, and the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>. Backed by Lusitanians and Celtiberians, Sertorius held off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Metellus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> for years. He was murdered by his officer Perperna in 73 or 72 BCE, and Pompey then ended the revolt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sertorian-war.jpg" alt="Sertorian War" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaume Noguera, Pau Valdés, and Eduard Ble · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sertorian_war.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During Sulla's civil war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> fought for the Marian-Cinnan faction, which sent him to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 83 BCE. When his side lost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, a Sullan army drove him out in 81 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where he helped the inhabitants depose the pro-Sullan tyrant Ascalis, he received an embassy of Lusitanians asking him to lead them against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s government, which had remembered his mild rule. Philip Spann argues that the Lusitanians hoped for a regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sympathetic to them rather than independence, and that Sertorius accepted because he could expect no amnesty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Sertorius landed at Baelo in 80 BCE, defeated Fufidius at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a> and, with his legate Lucius Hirtuleius, won control of both Hispanian provinces by 77 BCE, wearing down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-perperna-vento">Marcus Perperna</a> joined him in 76 BCE with fifty-three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, the remnant of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus's army. The Senate sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, who had never held a magistracy, with 30,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry, but Sertorius defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lauron">Battle of Lauron</a> in 76 BCE.</p><p>The year 75 BCE turned the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> defeated Perperna and Gaius Herennius at Valentia, where Herennius died among 10,000 casualties, and Metellus beat Hirtuleius at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a>, where Hirtuleius lost 20,000 men. Sertorius fought Pompey to a draw at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Sucro</a> and the combined Sullan armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, then withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clunia">Clunia</a> and rebuilt his army. Reinforced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Metellus and Pompey waged a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> from 74 BCE. Metellus offered a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and twenty thousand acres to any Roman who betrayed Sertorius, and when Sertorius replaced his Roman bodyguard with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, Roman officers began to plot against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Perperna invited Sertorius to a feast celebrating a supposed victory and had him murdered there, usually dated 72 BCE, though there are strong arguments for 73. Many Iberian allies made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> or Metellus or went home, and Pompey soon defeated Perperna by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> and ambush at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Osca</a>. In the view of the historian Scullard, Pompey treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> humanely by the Roman standard for rebels: many supporters received citizenship, and a group of determined opponents was resettled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum">Lugdunum Convenarum</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. The war is remembered for Sertorius's use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sertorian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final phase came in the <b>Sertorian War</b> of 80–72 BCE, when the Roman rebel Quintus Sertorius won the support of Lusitanians and Celtiberians and resisted the armies of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius and Pompey for several years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
